{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>וַיֵּרָא אֵלָיו ה׳ בְּאֵלֹנֵי מַמְרֵא.</b> It does not say 'וַיֵּרָא ה׳ אֶל אַבְרָהָם,' because his name and his essence were two opposites: for his name 'אַבְרָהָם,' after 'אַב הֲמוֹן גּוֹיִם,' signifies dominion and exaltation, while his essence was humble above all, as indicated by what he said: ״וְאָנֹכִי עָפָר וָאֵפֶר״ (Genesis 18:27). And so that one should not say that the Holy One, blessed be He, appears more to the great on account of their greatness, therefore it did not say 'וַיֵּרָא ה׳ אֶל אַבְרָהָם' — to indicate that it was not by virtue of his being אַב הֲמוֹן גּוֹיִם that YHWH appeared to him, but rather by virtue of his essence, for he was humble and lowly of spirit. And as our Sages, of blessed memory, said (Shabbat 92a): 'The Holy One, blessed be He, rests His Presence only upon one who is wise, mighty, rich, and humble.' Therefore it says ״וַיֵּרָא אֵלָיו״ — for his essence, which signifies humility, and not for his name, which signifies the opposite." "Another matter, the reverse of this: for at first YHWH appeared to him by virtue of his being Abram — אָב לַאֲרָם, because he brought them under the wings of the Presence, and it is as though he begot them. And because he was a father to all of them, YHWH appeared to him; but as for his essence, he was not yet worthy to behold the face of the Presence, because he was uncircumcised and blemished. But now that he was circumcised, YHWH appeared to him even by virtue of his essence. And to indicate this — that this appearance was on account of the circumcision — He appeared to him בְּאֵלוֹנֵי מַמְרֵא, for according to Rashi it was there that He gave him counsel concerning the circumcision. And it appears that Rashi learned this from the fact that ״בְּאֵלוֹנֵי״ is in the plural — for was he at that time amid many oaks? It should have said ״בְּאֵלוֹן מַמְרֵא״, for even though Mamre had many oaks and Abraham's tents were spread throughout all of them, nevertheless it would have sufficed had the radiance of the splendor of the Presence, blessed be He, spread through that one oak in which Abraham himself dwelt. Rather, certainly because Mamre had some merit and share in the commandment of circumcision, therefore YHWH appeared in all his oaks. And for this reason it also says ״וַיֵּרָא אֵלָיו״, so that you should not say that perhaps all the homeborn of Abraham's house were in those oaks and all were circumcised, and therefore the light of YHWH shone upon them all and not on account of Mamre — a hermeneutic teaches: ״וַיֵּרָא אֵלָיו״ — HaShem, to exclude all the homeborn and those acquired with his money. And if so, certainly the merit of Mamre caused it, as stated." "And it is possible to say that בְּאֵלוֹנֵי is not from the root of plainness (מִישׁוֹר) but rather a place where there were trees belonging to Mamre, and this is proven by what is said: ״וְהִשָּׁעֲנוּ תַּחַת הָעֵץ״ (Genesis 18:4), for there were many trees there. And our Sages, of blessed memory (Shabbat 108a), derived from here: whence do we learn that circumcision is in that place? עָרְלָה is said of trees and עָרְלָה is said here — just as there it refers to a place that produces fruit, so too circumcision is in a place that produces fruit. And so we find in the Midrash (Genesis Rabbah 46:5) that Abraham was in doubt about from where he should circumcise — whether from the mouth, or from the ear, or from the heart, etc. — therefore the light of HaShem appeared upon all the trees, as a sign showing him: look at these trees and learn from them, for the principal shining of the glory of HaShem is upon a place that produces fruit. And from this one learns the analogy: ״וּמִבְּשָׂרִי אֶחֱזֶה אֱלוֹהַּ״ (Job 19:26) — from a part of your flesh that resembles the trees. Yet in any case it is difficult: why did Scripture mention that these were trees belonging to Mamre? And further, how did Abraham act before the glory of HaShem shone upon him? Rather, certainly Mamre gave him counsel by reasoning that circumcision should be in a place that produces fruit, and HaShem agreed through his hand, for the glory of HaShem appeared to him in the oaks of Mamre. And with this everything is resolved, for the explanation is precious." "<b>וְהוּא יוֹשֵׁב פֶּתַח הָאֹהֶל כְּחֹם הַיּוֹם.</b> This teaches that from two sides he was worthy to behold the face of the Presence. The first is that he sat at the entrance of the tent to see whether wayfarers would come, and by virtue of charity a person merits and beholds the face of the Presence, as it is said (Psalms 17:15): ״אֲנִי בְּצֶדֶק אֶחֱזֶה פָנֶיךָ״. And with this the כְּ of כְּחֹם הַיּוֹם is resolved, for it should have said simply בְּחֹם הַיּוֹם; rather, he acted like the deed of the heat of the sun, which looks forward to perform charity, as it is said (Malachi 3:20): ״שֶׁמֶשׁ צְדָקָה וּמַרְפֵּא בִּכְנָפֶיהָ״, for through the lovingkindness of HaShem, blessed be He, the sun shines, as it is written (Psalms 136:7): ״לְעֹשֵׂה אוֹרִים גְּדֹלִים כִּי לְעוֹלָם חַסְדּוֹ״. The second: because he sat in the heat of the day to seek a sun of healing in its wings, therefore it was fitting that the glory of HaShem be revealed to him to visit the sick." "And what they said (Bava Metzia 86b): it was the third day after his circumcision, etc. — it appears that the author of this Midrash holds that Abraham was circumcised on Yom HaKippurim, as they derived (Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer 29) from the verse ״בְּעֶצֶם הַיּוֹם הַזֶּה״ (Genesis 17:26). And if so, perforce YHWH did not appear to him on the first day after the circumcision, for Abraham made a feast for the angels and fed them, and also the members of his household cooked and did work on that day — for according to the one who says it was Passover-eve and he fed them matzot, if so he certainly did not wish to prevent them from a commandment of HaShem. And if you should say that this day was the first day of Passover, in any case one does not cook on Yom Tov for the need of a non-Jew. And he was in doubt whether perhaps they were Arabs, so how did they cook for them and how did he feed them, since perhaps they were righteous? Rather, certainly on the first day after the circumcision YHWH did not appear to him. And if so, it is difficult: why did He not appear to him on the first day? Rather, certainly because the pain is not yet so great, and if so then also on the second day He did not appear to him — only on the third day, for then the pain is great, as they said (Genesis 34:25): ״וַיְהִי בַיּוֹם הַשְּׁלִישִׁי בִּהְיוֹתָם כֹּאֲבִים״. But from reasoning alone I would have said that they visited him even on the first day, even though the pain is not so great — rather, from the fact that the first day was Yom HaKippurim or Passover, we learn that He was particular specifically about the day when the pain is greatest." "<b>And he was sitting.</b> Rashi explained: it is written defectively [missing a vav]; he wished to stand, but the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: 'Sit, and you are a sign for your children,' etc. And every thoughtful person will marvel at the spectacle: what relevance does this sitting have to the sitting of judges, and why did HaShem show him such a thing specifically through this sitting? Therefore I say: since in this vision He mentioned no speech or commandment whatsoever, surely the purpose of this vision was to make known to Abraham the wickedness of the men of Sodom. And it was interrupted by the statement 'וְהִנֵּה שְׁלשָׁה אֲנָשִׁים נִצָּבִים עָלָיו,' that is, the receiving of guests — for through this Abraham would agree with the Holy One, blessed be He, to overturn Sodom, as is said of them (Ezekiel 16:49) 'הִנֵּה זֶה הָיָה עֲוֹן סְדוֹם אֲחוֹתֵךְ' etc. 'וְיַד עָנִי וְאֶבְיוֹן לֹא הֶחֱזִיקָה.' And it is known that a wayfarer does not come on foot [to Sodom], and the proof is from the angels, who did not wish to give them lodging. And the Holy One, blessed be He, wished to reveal to Abraham through the angels who chanced upon him — for it was revealed before Him, blessed be He, that they would not give the angels lodging, and therefore Abraham knew from this that they were legally liable for destruction. And it is plausible to hear that the Holy One, blessed be He, convened a heavenly court and an earthly court against the men of Sodom, to execute a joint judgment upon them from these two courts, as it is written 'וַה׳ הִמְטִיר עַל סְדוֹם' etc. 'מֵאֵת ה׳.' And wherever 'וַה׳' is written, it means He and His court, and therefore it says 'מֵאֵת ה׳,' that His court took that rain from HaShem. And Abraham He seated in the earthly court, for we find that the Holy One, blessed be He, seats the Patriarchs in judgment, as it is written (Micah 6:2) 'שִׁמְעוּ הָרִים אֶת רִיב ה׳ וְהָאֵתָנִים מוֹסְדֵי אָרֶץ.' And as our Sages of blessed memory said (Shabbat 89b): in the future the Holy One, blessed be He, will say to Abraham: 'Your children have sinned,' etc. And as it is written 'הַמְכַסֶּה אֲנִי מֵאַבְרָהָם' etc. 'כִּי יְדַעְתִּיו' etc. 'לַעֲשׂוֹת צְדָקָה וּמִשְׁפָּט.' For by this cause he would agree to their destruction, because they did not practice righteousness and justice, as was recounted regarding the false judges of Sodom (Sanhedrin 109a). Or one may say: since Abraham is the father of a multitude of nations, perhaps he would plead their merit to make a compromise, and this is 'righteousness and justice' — for any judgment that contains within it righteousness, you should say: this is a compromise [pesharah]. And after He seated Abraham in judgment against the men of Sodom, therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: 'Sit, and you are a sign for your children,' etc." "And what Rashi explained, that the Holy One, blessed be He, was standing while they were sitting — it appears he learned this from what is said 'וְהוּא יוֹשֵׁב,' for the word 'וְהוּא' appears superfluous, since it should have said 'וַיֵּשֶׁב אַבְרָהָם' at the beginning of the narrative, and afterward it should have said 'וַיֵּרָא אֵלָיו ה׳.' Rather, 'וְהוּא יוֹשֵׁב' comes to minimize the Shekhinah from sitting, for 'יֵשֵׁב,' lacking [a vav], carries in its meaning the force of the past — as though it said that he had sat previously but now did not sit — and it is also read 'יוֹשֵׁב' [present participle], and if so, everything is implied [by the deficient spelling]. And the reason for this is because our Sages of blessed memory said (Avot 4:22) that the Holy One, blessed be He, is the witness, He is the litigant, He is the judge. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, stood first, to inform us that He, blessed be He, is the litigant and the witness, for their manner is specifically in standing, as it is said (Micah 6:2) 'כִּי רִיב לַה׳ עִם עַמּוֹ וְעִם יִשְׂרָאֵל יִתְוַכָּח' — behold, He, blessed be He, is the litigant. And therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, stood in order that they might learn from Him that every litigant and witness conduct their proceedings standing, as they learned from the verse (Deuteronomy 19:17) 'וְעָמְדוּ שְׁנֵי הָאֲנָשִׁים אֲשֶׁר לָהֶם הָרִיב.' And afterward the Holy One, blessed be He, also became judge and sat with the judges, as it is said (Psalms 82:1) 'אֱלֹהִים נִצָּב בַּעֲדַת אֵל' — and this is at the time of argumentation and claims and testimony — and afterward 'בְּקֶרֶב אֱלֹהִים יִשְׁפֹּט,' He sits among them and judges. And this is a precious and honorable interpretation." "<b>In the heat of the day.</b> We have already said above that in this sitting the Holy One, blessed be He, seated Abraham in judgment, saying 'שִׁמְעוּ הָרִים אֶת רִיב ה׳' (Micah 6:2), for there is none who seeks out Elohim on earth. And we find that the judgment of the wicked is mostly in the morning, as it is said (Psalms 101:8) 'לַבְּקָרִים אַצְמִית כָּל רִשְׁעֵי אָרֶץ.' And so it is said of the men of Sodom (Genesis 19:23–24) 'הַשֶּׁמֶשׁ יָצָא עַל הָאָרֶץ וְלוֹט בָּא צֹעֲרָה, וַה׳ הִמְטִיר עַל סְדֹם.' And the reason for this is that we find that Abraham attained knowledge of HaShem, blessed be He, through the course of the sun and its movement." "And so it was explained in the Akeidah, the commentary on the book of Genesis, on the verse 'וְיָדַעְתָּ הַיּוֹם וַהֲשֵׁבֹתָ אֶל לְבָבֶךָ כִּי ה׳ הוּא הָאֱלֹהִים' (Deuteronomy 4:39): when you know the course of the daily sphere and its movements, you will recognize and know that YHWH is Elohim. And the Rav explained that this is the good signet that hung on Abraham's neck (Bava Batra 16b), for he would proclaim from his throat the existence of HaShem, blessed be He, and at his death he suspended this knowledge upon the sphere of the sun, as stated. If so — morning comes and the existence of HaShem is known to all creatures, and the wicked who did not know and did not understand the workings of YHWH and did not see the work of His hands — their own judgment brought about the morning, for the morning convicts them and cuts down the legs of the wicked. Therefore he said that Abraham had YHWH appear to him, as it is said 'וַיֵּרָא אֵלָיו ה׳,' and he was sitting at the entrance of the tent in the heat of the day, for he was meditating on the course of the sun that heats the day, and through investigation YHWH appeared to him. But the men of Sodom, who did not know YHWH, were judged at the going forth of the sun; therefore it says 'כְּחֹם הַיּוֹם,' for this is the matter upon which the decree against Sodom was sealed. Another matter: therefore 'כְּחֹם הַיּוֹם' was mentioned, for every judge must see it as though Gehinnom were open beneath him (Zohar Exodus 117a), and you already know that the expression 'כְּחֹם הַיּוֹם' is interpreted as referring to Gehinnom, as it is said (Malachi 3:19 [4:1]) 'וְלִהַט אוֹתָם הַיּוֹם הַבָּא' etc. And they learned from here that Abraham does not consign to Gehinnom any who are circumcised. Thus, according to our approach, this [phrase] is explained as referring to these judges."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 18, :notes {2 "The verse from Job 19:26 is cited in a truncated form with shifted word-order; I have translated the Hebrew citation exactly as the Kli Yakar quotes it rather than normalizing to the Masoretic text.", 5 "The phrase 'אינו מניח לגיהנם כל הנמולים' appears in comment [3], not [0]; noted here only as the supplied Hebrew for [0] contains no such phrase.", 6 "The Kli Yakar's wordplay on 'יֵשֵׁב' (defective spelling read as past) versus 'יוֹשֵׁב' (full participle) depends on the presence or absence of the letter vav in the word's spelling; the Hebrew text as given reads 'יֵשֵׁב' and 'יוֹשֵׁב' without distinguishing consonantal spelling, so the translation preserves both forms as they appear.", 8 "The Aramaic phrase 'רגליהון דרשיעי' is translated as 'the legs of the wicked,' preserving the idiom from the corresponding rabbinic source. 'Akeidah' refers to the Akeidat Yitzhak commentary by R. Isaac Arama."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:29:38.836622925Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}